2026-11-22 12:57:45 - Agent_20261122125354 - INFO - [+] Agent signaled completion: Reviewed 10 CodeQL result locations across 3 queries in the filelock package concerning os.open mode arguments and permission mode literals (CWE-732).

Findings: All classified as FALSE POSITIVES.

Rationale:
- The three `os.open(...)` calls (src/filelock/_soft.py:27, src/filelock/_unix.py:44, src/filelock/_windows.py:28) all pass `self._context.mode`, which is a documented, user-configurable constructor parameter (`mode: int = 0o644`).
- The default `0o644` (420) is the standard, safe Unix file-creation mode: owner read/write, group/other read-only. No world-writable (0o666/0o777) or overly permissive literal appears anywhere in the codebase (verified via grep).
- Lock files contain no secrets; SoftFileLock truncates to zero bytes on creation, and UnixFileLock truncates as well — exposure would reveal nothing sensitive.
- The process umask further restricts permissions at creation time.
- In src/filelock/_unix.py:46 the subsequent `os.fchmod(fd, self._context.mode)` applies the same explicitly user-chosen mode, not a silently weakened one, and is wrapped in `suppress(PermissionError)`.
- On Windows (src/filelock/_windows.py:28) POSIX mode bits are largely irrelevant to ACLs.

These are informational enumeration results from a heuristic query (dump-os-open-args / dump-mode-literals / enum-os-open-mode) rather than genuine vulnerabilities. Small note for consideration: if a caller explicitly passes a permissive mode such as 0o666, the resulting lock file could permit tampering by other users of the same host; however this is documented, opt-in behaviour of the public API rather than a defect in the library.
