2026-11-22 12:58:07 - Agent_20261122125354 - INFO - [+] Agent signaled completion: Reviewed all 4 results from the `filelock/fcntl-flock-audit` query. These are audit/observability findings that merely flag the presence of file-locking syscalls (fcntl.flock in src/filelock/_unix.py:48 and :63, msvcrt.locking in src/filelock/_windows.py:34 and :45) — which is the entire documented purpose of the py-filelock library. Investigation confirmed: (1) no untrusted or attacker-controlled data flows into the file descriptor or lock mode arguments; the fd comes from os.open() on self.lock_file, an internal Path attribute resolved by the library's own _acquire path selection; (2) the lock flags are constant values (LOCK_EX|LOCK_NB, LOCK_UN, LK_NBLCK, LK_UNLCK) with no injection surface; (3) the code correctly handles failure paths — closing the fd on OSError, distinguishing ENOSYS/EACCES, and intentionally not unlinking the lockfile on POSIX to avoid the well-known flock race (references issue #31 and the StackOverflow discussion in comments); (4) the Windows _release unlink is guarded by suppress(OSError). These are legitimate, correct implementations of OS advisory locking primitives, not vulnerabilities. All 4 classified as false positives.
